-A route reads the configuration through `locals.config()`, which reads `config/` from disk at most once a request and only when asked, and a server module takes it from its route as an argument rather than reading it itself, so an edit to `config/` reaches every route on the next request, in production too.
+A route reads the configuration through `locals.config()`, which reads `config/` from disk at most once a request and only when asked, and a server module takes it from its route as an argument rather than reading it itself, so an edit to `config/` reaches every route on the next request, in production too. The one server module that takes the configuration from the `config` channel instead is `server/root/live-settings.ts`, which has no route.
+
+The pollers are built from the configuration read when the server starts, and every config push hands them the settings read again through `server/root/live-settings.ts`, each group by its own reader: the Live HPC settings, the calendar settings, the mail settings, the usage refresh and the usage tiers, with the three sentences the pollers write on a row. A group that a hand edit makes unreadable keeps the settings in force and is logged once. `local.yml` is read when the server starts and used from that value, except the account lists and folders of the calendar and the mail, and `apps_dir`, which the apps section says is followed; its watch reads those again.
 
